103
00:08:57,320 --> 00:09:04,360
 Question number five, which method of sterilization is least likely to dull and corrode carbide

104
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:06,240
 instruments?

105
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:11,680
 Go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this.

106
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:18,000
 Alright so this is a nice little infection control question here, corrosion is a natural

107
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,560
 chemical process where the metal deteriorates, it forms an oxide, and it's usually caused

108
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:31,120
 by contact with an acid, but it could be something like water or some other liquid.

109
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:39,320
 Now in the case of steam heat, we're essentially putting these instruments through a pressurized

110
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:47,080
 sauna, ethylene oxide is also very humid, it's not as hot, but it does have a high moisture

111
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:54,880
 content, and glutaraldehyde, well we're essentially bathing instruments in this liquid.

112
00:09:54,880 --> 00:10:03,400
 Now dry heat is the only one here that has very low, low moisture, as the name suggests,

113
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:08,840
 and has the best preservation of cutting edges as a result.

114
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:15,800
 So if we didn't know specifically this high yield fact, we could try to group things together,

115
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:21,720
 see if we can get three of them on the same page, in this case three methods that involve

116
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:27,480
 high humidity or moisture, and find the one that doesn't belong, and in this case dry

117
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:30,960
 heat is the best example of that.

118
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:36,360
 So the best preservation of cutting instruments and the least likely to dull and corrode is

119
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:41,920
 the dry heat method of sterilization.

120
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:48,520
 Alright question number six, what is an example of a conditioned stimulus for a dental patient

121
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:53,480
 that had a previous bad experience at the dentist?

122
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:58,080
 So go ahead, pause the video, and then we'll go over this together.

123
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:06,960
 Alright so this is a good question looking at the concept of classical conditioning.

124
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:12,800
 So the example I use in dentistry is, the dentist gives you an injection, which is an

125
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:19,700
 unconditioned stimulus, but it's painful and causes anxiety, which is an unconditioned

126
00:11:19,700 --> 00:11:22,680
 natural stimulus for somebody.

127
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:29,440
 Now there's also a neutral stimulus present, which is the presence of the dentist who gives

128
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:35,320
 the injection, and that dentist is usually wearing a white coat to give said injection.

129
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:40,880
 So once this is repeated multiple times, that patient is getting multiple injections from

130
00:11:40,880 --> 00:11:47,160
 a dentist with a white coat, the mere presence of the dentist will eventually cause anxiety

131
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:54,040
 for the patient by itself, because now we're linking that previously neutral stimulus,

132
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:58,800
 and now the dentist is becoming the conditioned stimulus.

133
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:03,760
 But if we're being honest, there are multiple conditions stimuli that are appearing in this

134
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:04,760
 example.

135
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:10,240
 Not only the dentist administering the injection and the white coat they're wearing, but also

136
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:14,840
 just going to the dental office, waiting in the waiting room, getting called back and

137
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:19,360
 sitting in the dental chair, these are all things that are happening every time you're

138
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:24,320
 experiencing the injection or the drilling, whatever really bothers you.

139
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:28,600
 And so all of these things become conditioned stimuli.

140
00:12:28,600 --> 00:12:33,520
 Now with that in mind, well, there's really only one answer that makes sense here for

141
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:36,600
 a conditioned stimulus.

142
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:42,920
 High blood pressure, intrinsic fear, anxiety of a parent, these are all things that are

143
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:50,400
 more internalized fears, they're not linked to what's actually going on behind the scenes.

144
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:57,160
 But the dental chair, like in our example, is something that's in the background of your

145
00:12:57,160 --> 00:13:03,480
 experience, it's in the circumstance and you're subconsciously linking that to this feeling

146
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:10,080
 of pain and subsequent anxiety from the dental injection or whatever it might be.

147
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:16,400
 And so in this example, dental chair makes the most sense for an example for a conditioned

148
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:20,560
 stimulus.

149
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:27,800
 And here's our example of Pavlov's dogs that we went over in detail in our behavior change

150
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:29,360
 video.

151
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:37,240
 All right, question number seven, which of the following is not included in the ADA code

152
00:13:37,240 --> 00:13:39,040
 of conduct?

153
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:42,180
 Go ahead, pause the video and then we'll go over this together.

154
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:49,960
 All right, so this one is actually a bit tricky, but I see this concept come up all the time.

155
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:54,120
 So I definitely wanted to include it in our practice set.

156
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:59,080
 So remember, the principle of ethics is our list of five principles.

157
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:06,480
 And the code of conduct is the demonstration of those five principles in day to day dental

158
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:10,080
 practice.

159
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:14,680
 So let's go through these one by one licensure by credentials.

160
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:21,080
 So this is essentially the requirements for getting a dental license, very state to state.

161
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:29,440
 Not really sure about this one, let's come back to it advertising fees and patient care.

162
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:38,120
 Now the dentists, dentists must not falsely represent care being rendered, fees being charged,

163
00:14:38,120 --> 00:14:41,840
 or advertising of their dental practice.

164
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:50,480
 These are scenarios where the dentist is ethically required to be truthful for the patient and

165
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:52,400
 the community at large.

166
00:14:52,400 --> 00:15:00,840
 So these are all examples of the ethical principle of veracity or truthfulness.

167
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:05,560
 So those are certainly included in the code of conduct and they actually are if you went

168
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:09,700
 on the ADA website, you looked at the code of conduct, there are specific subsections

169
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:12,240
 for each of these.

170
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:13,320
 Now what does slander mean?

171
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:18,440
 That means you're saying something bad about another dentist or another dental professional.

172
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:23,480
 This is also something we should not be doing under the code of conduct.

173
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:30,720
 This is an ethical requirement not to slander another dental professional.

174
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:35,600
 And this is under the ethical principle of justice or fairness.

175
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:38,160
 So that is also included.

176
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:43,040
 Now licensure by credentials, as I alluded to before, it varies from state to state.

177
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:45,240
 It's not really an ethical concern.

178
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:51,520
 There are certain requirements that you need to uphold and meet, but they're not under

179
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:53,800
 the five ethical principles.

180
00:15:53,800 --> 00:16:02,040
 So the answer here is A.

181
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:04,560
 Alright question number eight.

182
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:09,920
 Which technique is typically not useful in treating an anxious patient?

