00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:15,200
 Alright, so go ahead pause the video and then we'll go over this together.

184
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:23,560
 Alright, so let's go through the answer choices because these are a little bit longer.

185
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:30,040
 A is using less structure in establishing rapport, B reassuring the patient by telling

186
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:36,960
 the patient not to worry, C providing reasons before asking for sensitive information,

187
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:42,400
 using empathy or making expectations clear.

188
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:46,960
 This is a little bit tricky if you're just looking at it and kind of thinking well they

189
00:16:46,960 --> 00:16:48,440
 all sound pretty good, right?

190
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:54,840
 Like all of these would potentially help an anxious patient out using less structure in

191
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:57,640
 establishing rapport.

192
00:16:57,640 --> 00:17:03,000
 So that's kind of talking about well we really want to just be open with the patient, try

193
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:08,040
 to get to know them, write rapport is all about establishing that initial level of trust.

194
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:16,120
 And an anxious patient, trust is incredibly important, potentially someone who's an anxious

195
00:17:16,120 --> 00:17:20,920
 patient like we talked about in one of our previous practice questions.

196
00:17:20,920 --> 00:17:24,280
 They could have had a bad experience, they could have had a previous dentist where things

197
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:29,120
 really didn't go well and so they might have a lot of questions, a lot of reservations,

198
00:17:29,120 --> 00:17:33,820
 a wall built up between you and the patient.

199
00:17:33,820 --> 00:17:39,280
 So rapport and taking time to get to know them can be a really useful trick.

200
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:42,560
 So I think that's a great thing to do.

201
00:17:42,560 --> 00:17:46,320
 See providing reasons before asking for sensitive information.

202
00:17:46,320 --> 00:17:49,520
 This is another thing that's providing trust, right?

203
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:53,840
 You're giving somebody, you're not just asking personal information from the patient right

204
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:58,400
 off the bat, you're letting them know well hey we really need to know this for comprehensiveness

205
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:02,680
 of care and we really appreciate any input you might have.

206
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:07,200
 Using empathy is one of those answers that you can never really say that's not a good

207
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:08,200
 thing.

208
00:18:08,200 --> 00:18:10,080
 Empathy is always a great thing to use.

209
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:15,600
 And making expectations clear is another one of those kind of, yeah that sounds good,

210
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:20,480
 that's something I can't really argue against that, I can't really say that's not a useful

211
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:23,440
 thing to treat an anxious patient.

212
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:27,960
 So the only thing that really sticks out to me is reassuring the patient by telling them

213
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:35,560
 not to worry and don't worry is a classic trap response.

214
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:39,800
 That's a really easy thing for a dentist to say oh don't worry it'll be fine, you'll be

215
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:41,240
 just fine.

216
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:48,440
 And providing this premature reassurance can actually compromise trust and rapport with

217
00:18:48,440 --> 00:18:49,640
 the patient.

218
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:54,360
 If it's not consistent with what actually happens, right, if they're actually in a lot

219
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:59,720
 of pain and it wasn't easy and they didn't feel fine, then they're not going to trust

220
00:18:59,720 --> 00:19:01,640
 you next time you say something like that.

221
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:10,000
 So it's better to be upfront with them, establish rapport, make them know that you're in it

222
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:19,280
 for them, right, you're not trying to be standoffish, you're actually trying to help them, you're

223
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:21,360
 not trying to hurt them.

224
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:25,640
 And so if they know you're on their side, but they also know that the appointment's not

225
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:31,160
 necessarily going to be enjoyable, then they're more likely to be on your team.

226
